meta {
  name: Participant - Blocked: Insert Round
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/rest/v1/rounds
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  apikey: {{anon_key}}
  Authorization: Bearer {{participant_jwt}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "event_id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
    "name": "Should Be Rejected",
    "sequence": 1,
    "is_final_round": false,
    "advancement_n": 1
  }
}

script:post-response {
  test("participant insert is rejected (4xx)", function () {
      expect(res.status).to.be.within(400, 499);
  });
}

docs {
  QA2 RLS: rounds_insert_organizer only allows the event organizer to insert. A participant attempting to insert a round must be rejected. Proves the participant role cannot write event structure.

  Note on status code: against this placeholder nil event id, the actual rejection comes from T8a's enforce_round_draft_only BEFORE INSERT trigger (400 check_violation) rather than RLS's WITH CHECK (403) — that trigger does its own defensive `select status from events where id = event_id` lookup, finds no row, treats the resulting NULL status as "not draft", and raises before RLS's own check ever runs. The write is still correctly blocked either way, so this asserts "rejected" (any 4xx) rather than a specific code. Pointed at a real draft-status event instead, RLS's 403 would be what fires.
}
